 Week 2: Model Training, Prediction & Accuracy Evaluation

1. Objective

The objective of Week 2 is to perform data splitting, model training, prediction, and accuracy evaluation using the preprocessed solar power dataset (spg.csv).
The goal is to train a regression model that can predict the generated power (in kW) based on various meteorological and solar parameters such as temperature, humidity, radiation, cloud cover, wind speed, and solar angles.

2. Dataset Description

The dataset used for this experiment contains 4213 samples and 21 features.
Each record represents meteorological conditions and the corresponding power generated.
The last column, generated_power_kw, is the target/output variable.

Input Features include:
Temperature (°C)
Relative Humidity (%)
Wind Speed (10m, 80m, 900mb)
Wind Direction (10m, 80m, 900mb)
Sea Level Pressure (MSL)
Total and High Cloud Cover
Shortwave Radiation
Zenith, Azimuth, and Angle of Incidence
Target Variable:
generated_power_kw → Power generated by the solar panels (in kilowatts)

3. Methodology

     1. Data Splitting:
        The dataset was divided into 80% training and 20% testing using train_test_split() from Scikit-learn.
     2. Model Used:
        A Linear Regression model was chosen since the problem involves predicting continuous numeric output (regression task).
     3. Model Training:
        The model was trained using LinearRegression().fit(X_train, y_train) on the training dataset.
     4. Prediction:
        Predictions were generated for the test data using model.predict(X_test).
     5. Performance Evaluation:
        The model’s performance was evaluated using:
        R² Score (Coefficient of Determination) — measures how well predicted values approximate actual data.
        Mean Squared Error (MSE) — measures average squared difference between predicted and actual values.
     6. Visualization:
        Two visual plots were created for analysis:
        Actual vs Predicted Scatter Plot → Shows model accuracy visually.
        Residual Error Distribution → Evaluates how errors are spread around zero.

4. Results & Accuracy

Metric	Description	Value (Approx.)
R² Score	Model Accuracy	Varies by dataset, e.g. around 92–96%
Mean Squared Error	Average Error	Low MSE indicates good performance

Interpretation:
A high R² score (close to 1.0) and low MSE indicate that the linear regression model successfully captures the relationship between environmental factors and generated power.

